90:* ''K15-H: the Handle owner rides the anchor'' (a new lane, scheduled after Wave C beside Wave D where the file sets prove disjoint; K18 owns the recovery door, not this). Scope: Handle founding, burn and rotation take the owner prefix from the sealed anchor through `readFace`, and `headOpKey` goes behind the door on both shores. The census (`face-reading-census.test.ts`) names the files by spelling until they migrate: `commands/handle.ts`, `browser-handle-verbs.ts` and `handle-publish.ts`. Read 4's two findings, verbatim. FINDING 1: MEASURED — `packages/lararium-mesh/src/handle-publish.ts:25-46`; `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/handle.ts:47-79`; `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-handle-publish.ts:23-40` — Handle founding takes its owner Persona KEL prefix straight from a mutable daemon tiddler, outside `readFace`. The seed is loaded by selected handle index, but `publishHandleFromDaemonDoc` never checks the selected face's sealed anchor, signed founding act, or verified KEL lineage. A daemon-document change can choose a foreign or stale prefix for the signed public Handle inception. The downstream Handle chain treats the owner prefix as part of its own signed inception, so the local signer can permanently publish the wrong owner binding even when the face boot would reject that selector. The census contains none of `publishHandleFromDaemonDoc`, `PERSONA_KEL_PREFIX_TIDDLER`, or `publishPersonaGlamour` in its primitive or decision-site lists. FINDING 2: MEASURED — `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/handle.ts:224-235,269-281`; `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-handle-verbs.ts:123-141` — owner burn and Handle rotation resolve owner authority from the mutable daemon prefix through a separate `headOpKey` verifier, outside `readFace`. These commands take `PERSONA_KEL_PREFIX_TIDDLER` from the daemon doc, resolve its board chain, and accept a matching root seed as the authorizing hand. The check proves that *some* chain has that current key; it does not prove that this Handle's owner member prefix is the selected face's sealed anchor prefix or the Handle inception's owner set. The Handle KEL's own membership verifier may still reject a mismatched owner prefix; the direct authority decision at the local command is nonetheless outside the door, and the census misses the entire `headOpKey` spelling. The same raw-prefix pattern serves both Node and browser.
92:* ''Read 5's remaining MEDIUMs'' (5 HIGH cured in the lane, MEDIUMs named, none authority-bearing past the proof): the admit preflight checks that a frontier record exists but leaves its signed hand, group, roster and snapshot to the later boot's reader (`ceremony-core.ts`); the issuer stamps the signed admission's epoch from the raw chain head, where the verified lineage's last epoch would match (`device-admit.ts`); the fleet-join offer reads its group through `facePersonaGroup` from the bootstrap on the founding face (`fleet-join.ts`) and `init.ts` reads the bootstrap group to choose `alreadyStood`; a holder session whose lock or proof rejects after the greeting leaves queued asks waiting without a named refusal (`shared-holder.ts`); the browser's second face neither joins nor proves its identities and circles documents (Wave D slice 4). Each rides its owning lane's row: K17 for the frontier verification, K15-H for the Handle owner, Wave D for the browser faces.
93:* ''Read 6's two lane-owned HIGH, written verbatim as Wave D's opening rows'' (read 6 found no P0: an unaffiliated wire peer reached no face act and no private plane). ROW 1: MEASURED — lane-owned composition, foreign face share falls open when its catalog row is absent. `packages/lararium-node/src/face-fleet-view.ts:76-87,131-137`; `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts:753-754`. One-line reason: the worn face bridge allows *every* document id outside a negative set derived from the mutable catalog, so deleting or withholding a foreign face's row before a new session is seated removes that face's private plane from the deny set. This is the exact inverse of the proposed authority direction: an unverified catalog *absence* changes a share from false to true. The `bb8513247` repair added `foreignFaceDocs`, but the live verdict is `view.fetch(id).ok || !others.has(id)`; a missing catalog entry makes `others.has` false. The source test itself demonstrates the relevant behavior at `face-fleet-view.test.ts:122-140`: with only the worn bootstrap, the bare bridge reads the other face's circles, and supplying a catalog-derived `foreign` set blocks it. The control never tries the catalog row deleted after registration, or a held other-face document whose catalog entry was omitted. A same-operator session of the worn face can fetch the other face's held document by id in that state. The verdict is wired through `shareConfigOf` to both the announce and access hooks (`federation-gate.ts:602-611`), so this is an access decision rather than an announcement-only cosmetic leak; the live transport control at `share-policy-is-access.test.ts:49-67` demonstrates that distinction. This is not P0 under the user's definition because the reader already has an admitted face; the leak crosses face compartments on one vessel. The authorization decision must come from the sealed/proven face-document inventory or a positive view, rather than catalog completeness. The broad bridge predates Wave C (Wave B/O1), but Wave C owns the `foreignFaceDocs` repair and its composer wiring; this finding concerns that lane-owned repair, not the inherited bridge in isolation. ROW 2: MEASURED — lane-owned node daemon keeps a lease floor older than the sealed anchor it raises at boot. `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts:2260-2271,2311-2346`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/operator-daemon-behavior.ts:306-321,421-423,704-743,986-1008`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/lease-frontier.ts:64-70`. One-line reason: the composer snapshots `standingAnchor` into `daemonAuth` and awaits `openDaemonVm` *before* it raises and persists the verified lease epoch; the running daemon's live join and grant reads keep flooring under the old object. For a concrete counterexample, start with sealed lease epoch 0 and a verified signed advance to 7 in the mirrored daemon doc. The daemon receives anchor 0, starts, then the parent persists anchor 7 at `:2346`. If that mutable slot is deleted or replayed to 0 during the same daemon lifetime, `readLeaseUnderFace` reads 0 and floors against the daemon's captured anchor 0, despite custody now pinning 7. Its live face-join at `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:704-743` passes the lowered `lease.n` into a new seat and re-seal, while grant ingest at `:306-321` verifies an older bound. A restart loads 7 and closes the window; the claim that the sealed frontier never falls in a standing daemon does not hold. This is Wave C's lease-observation and composer ordering, not an inherited timer. The seam should raise the anchor before forming/sending `daemonAuth`, or deliver the raised pin to the daemon through a verified update. The existing red at `signed-lease-frontier.test.ts:50-61` hands `readLeaseUnderFace` an anchor already at 7, and `lease-observation.test.ts:26` tests the pure raise, so both can pass while the composer sends 0 before it persists 7. A red needs the live daemon, a signed advance, a subsequent slot deletion, and a control that the older edge or grant remains refused without restart.
